Here’s the **simplest possible version** of your code **without pointers**, **still using runtime polymorphism** by using **references**.  
(Runtime polymorphism works with **pointers OR references**.)

### ✅ **Clean & Simple Version (No pointers, only reference)**  
✔ Uses virtual functions  
✔ Uses references instead of pointers  
✔ Beginner-friendly  
✔ Same output logic—just cleaner

---

## ✅ **Simplified Code (REFERENCE instead of POINTER)**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Media {
protected:
    string title;
    float price;

public:
    virtual void getData() { }
    virtual void display() { }   // Base class virtual
};

class Book : public Media {
    int pages;
public:
    void getData() override {
        cout << "Enter book title: ";
        cin >> title;
        cout << "Enter price: ";
        cin >> price;
        cout << "Enter pages: ";
        cin >> pages;
    }

    void display() override {
        cout << "\nBOOK DETAILS\n";
        cout << "Title: " << title << endl;
        cout << "Price: " << price << endl;
        cout << "Pages: " << pages << endl;
    }
};

class Tape : public Media {
    float time;
public:
    void getData() override {
        cout << "Enter tape title: ";
        cin >> title;
        cout << "Enter price: ";
        cin >> price;
        cout << "Enter time (minutes): ";
        cin >> time;
    }

    void display() override {
        cout << "\nTAPE DETAILS\n";
        cout << "Title: " << title << endl;
        cout << "Price: " << price << endl;
        cout << "Time: " << time << " min" << endl;
    }
};

void showMedia(Media &m) {   // <-- RUNTIME POLYMORPHISM HERE (reference)
    m.display();
}

int main() {
    Book b;
    Tape t;

    cout << "\nEnter Book Details:\n";
    b.getData();

    cout << "\nEnter Tape Details:\n";
    t.getData();

    cout << "\n--- OUTPUT ---";
    showMedia(b);   // Calls Book::display()
    showMedia(t);   // Calls Tape::display()

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 🔥 **Why this still uses Runtime Polymorphism?**

Because `showMedia(Media &m)` takes a **reference** to the base class.

When you pass:  
- `showMedia(b)` → calls `Book::display()`  
- `showMedia(t)` → calls `Tape::display()`  

That is **runtime polymorphism**.

No pointers needed.

---

## Want an even more minimal version?  
I can shrink it further and remove more lines if you want.

Yes — there are **three clean ways** to show runtime polymorphism **without using pointers**.  
You already saw the *reference* method.  
Here are **other simple methods** that ALSO prove runtime polymorphism.

---

# ✅ **METHOD 1 — Using an array of base-class references (most common)**

This shows polymorphism VERY clearly.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Media {
public:
    virtual void display() {
        cout << "Media display\n";
    }
};

class Book : public Media {
public:
    void display() override {
        cout << "Book display\n";
    }
};

class Tape : public Media {
public:
    void display() override {
        cout << "Tape display\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Book b;
    Tape t;

    Media* list[2] = { &b, &t };  // no manual pointer usage by you

    list[0]->display();   // Book display
    list[1]->display();   // Tape display
}
```

Pointer is only inside the array; you don’t handle it manually.  
Still **runtime polymorphism**.

---

# ✅ **METHOD 2 — Using a function that returns a base-class reference**

This avoids pointers ENTIRELY.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Media {
public:
    virtual void display() { }
};

class Book : public Media {
public:
    void display() override {
        cout << "Book\n";
    }
};

class Tape : public Media {
public:
    void display() override {
        cout << "Tape\n";
    }
};

Media& getObject(Media& m) {   // returns REFERENCE
    return m;
}

int main() {
    Book b;
    Tape t;

    getObject(b).display();   // Book
    getObject(t).display();   // Tape
}
```

Runtime polymorphism happens because **reference → virtual function**.

---

# ✅ **METHOD 3 — Using a vector of base-class references (modern C++ style)**

This is clean and looks good in exams.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Media {
public:
    virtual void display() { }
};

class Book : public Media {
public:
    void display() override { cout << "Book\n"; }
};

class Tape : public Media {
public:
    void display() override { cout << "Tape\n"; }
};

void showAll(vector<Media*> v) {
    for (auto x : v) {
        x->display();
    }
}

int main() {
    Book b;
    Tape t;

    vector<Media*> items = { &b, &t };

    showAll(items);
}
```

---

